a') Thought: I have opened the image size settings dialog and now need to resize the image to 1cm by 1cm. I see that the current width and height are both 13.55cm. I need to change both values to 1cm. I should first click the width input box and enter the value 1cm. Then, I also need to set the height to 1cm.。 Action: click(start_box='(911,720)') Thought: I see that a presentation is currently open in LibreOffice Impress, and I need to add a 1cm by 1cm image named \"none.png\" to slide 2. First, I need to click the second slide in the thumbnail pane on the left. I should click the second slide thumbnail in the left panel to start editing. From the thumbnail, I can see that slide 2 contains some content about marketing strategies. Action: click(start_box='(86,365)') Thought: I have successfully selected slide 2. Now, I need to insert an image. In LibreOffice Impress, I can insert an image through the \"Insert\" option in the top menu bar. I should click the \"Insert\" menu at the top, then choose the option to insert an image in order to add our \"none.png\" picture. Action: click(start_box='(120,69)') Thought: I have opened the Insert menu and now need to select the \"Image...\" option to insert an image. This option is located at the top of the menu. I should click the \"Image...\" option under the Insert menu to open the file selection dialog and choose our \"none.png\" image.。 Action: click(start_box='(133,84)') Thought: I have opened the image insertion dialog and now need to navigate to the Desktop to select the \"none.png\" file. I see the Desktop option in the left navigation pane. I should click the Desktop option in the left sidebar to access the files on the desktop. Action: click(start_box='(239,264)') Thought: I have successfully navigated to the Desktop directory and can now see the \"none.png\" file. I need to select and open this file to insert it into the slide. I should double-click the \"none.png\" file to select it, then click the \"Open\" button to insert it into the slide. Action: left_double(start_box='(321,251)') Thought: I have successfully inserted the image into the slide and now need to resize it to 1cm by 1cm. In the right-side properties panel, I can see the \"Position and Size\" option. I need to click it to set the image size. I should click the \"Position and Size\" option in the right properties panel to open the image size settings dialog. Action: click(start_box='(830,628)') Thought: I have selected the width input box and now need to enter the new width value of 1cm. I should first delete the current value by pressing Ctrl+A to select all the text, then type in the new width value of 1cm. Action: hotkey(key='ctrl a') 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 Our Model Thought: I have selected all the text in the width input box and now need to enter the new width value of 1cm. I should simply type in the new width value, 1cm, to adjust the image width to 1cm. Action: type(content='1cm') Thought: I have successfully set the width to 1cm and now need to set the height to 1cm to complete the 1cm by 1cm image size adjustment. I should click the height input box and then enter the value 1cm. I need to click the height input box and type 1cm to finalize the image size setting. Action: click(start_box='(911,760)') Thought: I have selected all the text in the height input box and now need to enter the new height value of 1cm to complete the 1cm by 1cm image size adjustment. I should simply type in the new height value, 1cm, to finalize the image size setting. Action: type(content='1cm') Thought: I have successfully set the image size to 1cm by 1cm and now need to save these changes to complete the task. I should use the shortcut Ctrl+S to save the file. Using Ctrl+S will save the current modifications and ensure the image size settings are preserved. Action: hotkey(key='ctrl s') Thought: The task of arranging the event titled \"homework\" for May 21st with a notification time of 10 minutes before has been successfully completed. The event is visible on the calendar, and no further actions are required to fulfill the task. I will now submit the task as completed. Submit the task, confirming that it has been successfully completed. Action: finished() Thought: To proceed with arranging the event, I need to select the \"New event\" option from the pop-up menu. This option is specifically designed for creating new events, which is the next logical step in completing the task. Tap on the \"New event\" option in the pop-up menu to begin creating a new event. Action: click(start_box='(678,690)') Thought: To proceed with arranging the event, I need to open the Calendar app. This requires identifying and tapping on the Calendar app icon, which is clearly visible on the screen. Tap on the \"Calendar\" app icon to open the Calendar application. Action: click(start_box='(606,381)') Thought: To proceed with arranging the event, I need to initiate the process of creating a new event. The \"+\" button is the standard way to add a new event in most calendar applications, so tapping it will allow me to start entering the event details. Tap on the blue \"+\" button in the bottom right corner to begin creating a new event. Action: click(start_box='(357,254)') Thought: To proceed with arranging the event, I need to initiate the process of creating a new event. The \"+\" button is the standard way to add a new event in most calendar applications, so tapping it will allow me to start entering the event details. Tap on the blue \"+\" button in the bottom right corner to begin creating a new event. Action: click(start_box='(895,831)') Thought: To proceed with arranging the event, I need to input the title \"homework\" into the \"Event...\" field. This is the first step in defining the event's details, and entering the title is essential for identifying the event. Type \"homework\" into the \"Event...\" field to set the event's title. Action: type(content='homework') Thought: To proceed with arranging the event, I need to confirm or modify the notification time. Since the task specifies setting the notification time to 10 minutes before, I will tap on the \"9:00 AM\" field to access the time selection interface and make the necessary adjustment. Tap on the \"9:00 AM\" field to open the time selection interface for modifying the notification time. Action: click(start_box='(688,239)') 1 2 3 4 5 6 Base Model Thought: To set the notification time to 10 minutes before the event, I need to adjust the hour to 8:50 AM. The red circle indicates the target position on the clock face, which corresponds to the desired time. Tapping on this position will update the hour and minute accordingly. Tap on the \"8\" position on the clock face to set the hour to 8. Action: click(start_box='(288,616)') Thought: To set the notification time to 10 minutes before the event, I need to adjust the minute to 50. The red circle indicates the target position on the clock face, which corresponds to the desired time. Tapping on this position will update the minute accordingly. Tap on the \"50\" position on the clock face to set the minute to 50. Action: click(start_box='(288,503)')","level":1,"path_id":"1~26","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":28,"section_id":"sec-1~26"},{"title":"Thought:","text":"To finalize the notification time of 8:50 AM, I need to confirm the selection by tapping the \"OK\" button. This action will save the chosen time and return to the event creation interface, allowing me to continue setting up the event. Tap on the \"OK\" button to confirm the selected time of 8:50 AM. Action: click(start_box='(804,760)')","level":1,"path_id":"1~27","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":29,"section_id":"sec-1~27"},{"title":"Thought:","text":"To complete the task of arranging the event, I need to confirm the details and save the event. Tapping the \"X\" button will likely close the event creation interface, indicating that the event has been successfully created and saved. Tap on the \"X\" button in the top-left corner to confirm and save the event.Action: click(start_box='(67,87)') Thought: To proceed with arranging the event, I need to tap on the \"At time of event\" option to open the notification settings. This step is necessary to adjust the notification time to 10 minutes before the event, as specified in the task, and ensure the event is properly configured. Tap on the \"At time of event\" option to open the notification settings. Action: click(start_box='(428,307)') Our Model Thought: To proceed with arranging the event, I need to select the \"New event\" option from the pop-up menu. This will open the event creation interface, where I can input the event title, set the date, and configure the notification time as required. Tap on the \"New event\" option in the pop-up menu to begin creating a new event. Action: click(start_box='(892,690)') Thought: To proceed with arranging the event, I need to open the Calendar app. This will allow me to create a new event and set the necessary details, such as the title, date, and notification time. Clicking on the Calendar app is the logical next step to achieve this. Tap on the \"Calendar\" app icon to open the Calendar application. Action: click(start_box='(606,381)') Thought: To proceed with arranging the event, I need to select May 21st on the calendar. This will allow me to specify the date for the event and move forward with setting the title and notification time. Tap on the date \"May 21st\" in the calendar to select it for the event. Action: click(start_box='(357,254)') Thought: To proceed with arranging the event, I need to initiate the process of creating a new event. Clicking on the \"+\" button will allow me to access the event creation interface, where I can input the event title, set the date, and configure the notification time. Tap on the blue \"+\" button in the bottom right corner to start creating a new event. Action: click(start_box='(880,831)') Thought: To proceed with arranging the event, I need to tap on the \"Event...\" text field to activate it and input the title \"homework.\" This is the logical next step to ensure the event is properly labeled and aligns with the task requirements. Tap on the \"Event...\" text field to begin entering the event title. Action: click(start_box='(263,145)') Thought: To proceed with arranging the event, I need to type \"homework\" into the active text field. This step is essential to label the event appropriately and ensure it aligns with the task requirements. Type \"homework\" into the active \"Event...\" text field. Action: type(content='homework') 1 2 3 4 5 6 Thought: To proceed with arranging the event, I need to tap on the \"9:00 AM\" time field to open the time selection interface. This step is necessary to adjust the notification time to 10 minutes before the event, as specified in the task. Tap on the \"9:00 AM\" time field to open the time selection interface. Action: click(start_box='(688,238)') Thought: To proceed with arranging the event, I need to adjust the hour to \"10\" on the clock interface. This step is necessary to set the correct hour for the event, as specified in the task, and move closer to completing the time configuration. Tap on the \"10\" position on the clock interface to set the hour to 10. Action: click(start_box='(288,506)') Thought: To proceed with arranging the event, I need to adjust the minute to \"55\" on the clock interface. This step is necessary to set the correct time for the event, ensuring it aligns with the task requirement of setting the notification time to 10 minutes before the event. Tap on the \"55\" position on the clock interface to set the minute to 55. Action: click(start_box='(384,466)') Thought: To proceed with arranging the event, I need to confirm the selected time of \"10:55 AM\" by tapping the \"OK\" button. This step is essential to finalize the time configuration and move forward in completing the event setup. Tap on the \"OK\" button to confirm the selected time of \"10:55 AM.\" Action: click(start_box='(804,762)') 7 8 9 10 11 Thought: To proceed with arranging the event, I need to select the \"10 minutes before\" option in the notification settings. This step is essential to ensure the notification is set to the correct time, as specified in the task, and aligns with the requirement to set the notification time to 10 minutes before the event. Tap on the \"10 minutes before\" option to set the notification time to 10 minutes before the event. Action: click(start_box='(162,485)') Thought: To complete the task of arranging the event, I need to tap the \"SAVE\" button to confirm and save all the configured details, including the title, date, time, and notification settings. This step is essential to ensure the event is successfully created and aligned with the task requirements. Tap on the \"SAVE\" button to finalize and save the event details. Action: click(start_box='(812,926)') Thought: The task of arranging the event titled \"homework\" for May 21st with a notification time of 10 minutes before has been successfully completed. The event is now visible in the calendar, and all the required details have been configured correctly. I can now submit the task as completed. Submit the task, confirming that it has been successfully completed. Action: finished() 14 13 12","level":1,"path_id":"1~28","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":30,"section_id":"sec-1~28"},{"title":"Figure 2 :","text":"2Figure2: Top: Overview of ZeroGUI. It adopts a Two-stage Online Reinforcement Learning paradigm. In the first stage, tasks are automatically generated by a VLM, while in the second stage, tasks are drawn from the test set. These tasks are executed by the GUI agent. After each interaction, a reward is assigned automatically by the VLM based on the agent's trajectory, and the policy network is updated via reinforcement learning. Bottom left: Automatic Task Generation. The VLM receives a random initial screenshot and a set of task exemplars to generate diverse novel tasks. Bottom right: Automatic Reward Estimation. The final reward is obtained via majority voting of multiple VLM evaluations based on all screenshots of the trajectory.","level":1,"path_id":"1~29","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":31,"section_id":"sec-1~29"},{"title":"Figure 3 :","text":"3Figure 3: Comparison of training accuracies with k3-KL (GRPO) and k2-KL (ours).","level":1,"path_id":"1~30","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":32,"section_id":"sec-1~30"},{"title":"2 .","text":"2The proposed task should be similar to the example tasks but not exactly the same. 3. The {Number of Instructions} instructions should cover different aspects of using {App Name}. During the generation, think about any other kind of tasks differ from the examples you have generated before. 4. The tasks should be clear and unambiguous, with specific and detailed instructions, and can be evaluated objectively by observing the action trajectory. 5. If the task you propose requires access to any internet resources, you must ensure that the URL or resource exists. 6. The task you propose must require at least 3 steps to complete, and the difficulty and the complexity should be evenly distributed. The following are examples of good task instructions (their initial screenshots/states are different): {List of Exemplars} Please follow the **content, type, and distribution** of the examples provided to you, and generate task instructions based on the initial screenshot. **Do not repeat the existing examples.** Now, you will be given a initial computer state screenshot. Please generate {Number of Instructions} task instructions based on the screenshot. Return only the task instruction text. Each instruction should occupy a single line. {Number of Instructions} lines of instructions should be returned.\nFINAL ANSWER: The task was not completed successfully due to the lack of observable saving action. SCORE: 0 Now, please **strictly follow the format** and analyze the following screenshots (The last line should only be SCORE: [0/1], no other text): Task Instruction: {instruction} Screenshots (by order): {screenshots} A.3 RL Training Hyper-parameters of our RL training are listed in Tab. 5.","level":1,"path_id":"1~31","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":33,"section_id":"sec-1~31"},{"title":"Figure 4 :","text":"4Figure 4: Gradient coefficient of KL loss.","level":1,"path_id":"1~32","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":34,"section_id":"sec-1~32"},{"title":"Figure 5 :","text":"5Figure 5: KL loss curve and token-wise maximum and minimum of log π θ -log π ref during training.","level":1,"path_id":"1~33","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":35,"section_id":"sec-1~33"},{"title":"Figure 6 :","text":"6Figure 6: Test success rates with different KL loss coefficients β.","level":1,"path_id":"1~34","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":36,"section_id":"sec-1~34"},{"title":"Figure 7 :","text":"7Figure 7: Visualization of the test and generated task instructions in OSWorld.","level":1,"path_id":"1~35","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":37,"section_id":"sec-1~35"},{"title":"Figure 8 :","text":"8Figure 8: The trajectory of the base model UI-TARS-7B-DPO when executing a VS Code task. The instruction is \"I want the tabs to wrap onto multiple lines when they exceed the available space. Please help modify the VS Code settings accordingly.\" The original thoughts are in Chinese and have been translated into English for presentation purposes. Subsequent repetitive steps have been omitted.","level":1,"path_id":"1~36","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":38,"section_id":"sec-1~36"},{"title":"Figure 9 :","text":"9Figure 9: The trajectory of our trained model when executing a VS Code task. The instruction is \"I want the tabs to wrap onto multiple lines when they exceed the available space. Please help modify the VS Code settings accordingly.\" The original thoughts are in Chinese and have been translated into English for presentation purposes.","level":1,"path_id":"1~37","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":39,"section_id":"sec-1~37"},{"title":"Figure 10 :","text":"10Figure 10: The trajectory of the base model UI-TARS-7B-DPO when executing a LibreOffice Impress task. The instruction is \"Add an image 'none.png' on the Desktop to slide 2 with 1cm*1cm size.\" The original thoughts are in Chinese and have been translated into English for presentation purposes. Subsequent repetitive steps have been omitted.","level":1,"path_id":"1~38","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":40,"section_id":"sec-1~38"},{"title":"Figure 11 :","text":"11Figure 11: The trajectory of our trained model when executing a LibreOffice Impress task. The instruction is \"Add an image 'none.png' on the Desktop to slide 2 with 1cm*1cm size.\" The original thoughts are in Chinese and have been translated into English for presentation purposes.","level":1,"path_id":"1~39","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":41,"section_id":"sec-1~39"},{"title":"Figure 12 :","text":"12Figure 12: The trajectory of the base model UI-TARS-7B-DPO when executing a Calendar task. The instruction is \"You should use calendar to complete the following task: Arrange an event titled 'homework' for me at May 21st, and set the notification time to be 10 minutes before.\"","level":1,"path_id":"1~40","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":42,"section_id":"sec-1~40"},{"title":"Figure 13 :","text":"13Figure 13: The trajectory of our trained model when executing a Calendar task. The instruction is \"You should use calendar to complete the following task: Arrange an event titled 'homework' for me at May 21st, and set the notification time to be 10 minutes before.\"","level":1,"path_id":"1~41","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":43,"section_id":"sec-1~41"},{"title":"Table 1 :","text":"1Test results on OSWorld benchmark. Test settings and metrics are described in Sec. 4.1. The success rates 